  "query_preview": "..\n(This afternoon through Sunday)\nIssued at 203 PM CDT MON OCT 26 2026\n\nThis afternoon a large high pressure ridge extends from New \nEngland, into the Deep South, and westward into Texas. In the \nupper levels, a ridge is centered over the Deep South with large \nscale troughing over the Western US and a second low over the \nCaribbean/troughing just off the Atlantic Coast. Dry conditions \nwill persist through Friday with warm days and cool nights. A \nsurface front is stretched across New York to Illinois to Nebraska\ntoday with a dry line extending southwestward into New Mexico. \nUpper ridging will remain across the Southeast US into Tuesday \nbefore shifting eastward on Wednesday. We will remain initially in\nan overall rain free pattern with limited available moisture for \nrain, but high cirrus will likely continue at times through \nWednesday with some minor perturbations in the upper flow. \nHowever, the lower levels will still be too dry during the week \nfor rain with no major frontal system set to move through the area\nthrough the forecast. Temperatures will remain fairly similar \nthrough mid week with lows in the 40s to the lower 50s and highs \ngenerally in the 70s across Central Alabama. Toward the end of the\nweek, our low level flow begins to turn more east-southeast. This\nwill allow for some moderating of our airmass with 50s and a few \n60s and some lower 80s mingling in with the respective lows and \nhighs by Thursday into this weekend. Rain chances return by\nSaturday as overall moisture increases.\n\n08",
